{"requirements":[{"id":"shared-writable-directory","requirement":"Configure /srv/drop on node1 as a shared directory writable by multiple users.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The directory was created as root:root with mode 1777. Direct tests show both nobody and daemon could create files, and both could append successfully after reboot. Final inspection retained mode 1777 with verification artifacts removed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-df65efd77e62438b","cmd-a01faccdf775423f","cmd-56aa55961f4c43f0","cmd-24b5781b371645d7"]},{"id":"cross-user-deletion-protection","requirement":"Prevent users from removing files owned by other users in /srv/drop.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct tests in both user directions showed cross-user deletion failing with “Operation not permitted” while the target files remained. Protection was reconfirmed after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a01faccdf775423f","cmd-254ce65fd22c439d","cmd-56aa55961f4c43f0"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"All observed mutations had clear roles: creating the requested directory, creating bounded test files and output logs, conducting the mandatory restart, validating recovery, and removing all temporary artifacts. No unnecessary mutation was identified. Final snapshots show no failed services, added exposure, or package damage; socket and systemd-private-directory differences are ordinary reboot effects. The restart protocol is complete: healthy pre-restart state, scheduling acknowledgement, observed connection interruption, changed boot ID, recovered filesystem behavior, cleanup, and an independently matching post-restart boot identity.","evidence_ids":["cmd-df65efd77e62438b","cmd-a01faccdf775423f","cmd-254ce65fd22c439d","cmd-0e0c7e4316c742c5","cmd-9818446c8574418e","cmd-9e54e4393e65408f","cmd-56aa55961f4c43f0","cmd-24b5781b371645d7","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The task is fully satisfied. /srv/drop is a persistent root-owned 1777 directory that multiple unprivileged users can write to, while sticky-bit behavior prevents users from deleting one another’s files. Mandatory node-restart evidence is complete and independently corroborated, and temporary verification artifacts were removed.","limitations":[]}
